# The diagnostics of the BORROWING family: the lend, the field lend and the
# lease (design.md §4.19, §4.20; panels 121, 122, 124, 164).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE. `ffi_errors.hero` answers one question — what may
# CROSS the boundary — and these five answer a different one: what a pointer
# already across may DO, and where it may stand. The checker has carried that
# seam since panel 124 (`check/lending.hero` and `check/leasing.hero`, and
# `check/lend_types.hero` since panel 164) and the diagnostics had not followed.
# Panel 164's two new codes pushed the host past §11's 300 lines — `layout` said
# 302 — and this is the cut, rather than a raised ceiling.
#
# NO `Fix` ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, and that is a decision rather than an
# omission: every repair here is a different SHAPE of program — bind the record
# first, answer the `str` and let the caller lend it — and `.claude/rules/
# diagnostics-and-goldens.md` says a fix that leaves the defect standing is a
# guess however well it compiles.

use token
use diag

# A lent C pointer standing anywhere but in the call that reads it
# (defect 022, found by the ffi-pragmatist at panel 121; author decision
# 2026-09-09 for the strict rule over the escape-only one).
#
# ONE CODE AND NOT TWO, though the reader arrives by two roads. A lend
# built from an expression is a use-after-free and a lend built from a
# literal is sound, because a literal has static storage; the message
# does not split them, because the two spellings are one brace apart and
# a rule that held only for one of them would teach the difference
# instead of the rule. The note carries the repair, which is a different
# shape of program rather than a different expression, so there is no
# `Fix` here: nothing mechanical turns an escaping pointer into a
# correct one.
#
# THREE SENTENCES OF THIS DIAGNOSTIC WERE WRONG UNTIL PANEL 122, and the
# spec-warden and the ffi seat caught them separately. It said "not an
# argument of an EXTERN call", from a rule the sitting dropped. It said
# the bytes are "released when the function that built it returns",
# which is false in general — the slot is released on re-execution too,
# so a loop frees the previous iteration — and false of every site where
# it then fired, which were literals. And its note prescribed writing
# `.cstr()` inside the extern call, which `extern_across_modules`
# refuses: the two diagnostics pointed at each other, and a reader
# following either arrived at the other.
function cstr_escapes(span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "cstr_escapes",
        message: "`.cstr()` lends this string to C for the length of one call (§4.19), and a lend may stand only as an argument of a call — here it stands somewhere the pointer outlives the bytes it points into",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "pass it directly as an argument of the call that reads it. The bytes belong to the `str`, and the slot holding one is released when its function returns OR when the same expression runs again, whichever comes first — so a lend kept past its call is reading freed memory by the next iteration. A wrapper that reaches C from another module takes a `str` and lends inside itself"
    )
    return d

# The same position rule, over a FIELD's lend (panel 164).
#
# IT IS A SECOND DIAGNOSTIC AND NOT A WIDENING OF THE ONE ABOVE, because that
# one says `str` four times and every sentence of it would be false here. A
# field's bytes belong to the RECORD, not to a `str`, and they die when the
# record's slot does rather than when a runtime string is released — so the
# repair a reader needs is a different sentence, and §4.17 asks a diagnostic to
# carry what fixes the program without opening another file.
function field_lend_escapes(span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "field_lend_escapes",
        message: "`.ptr()` lends this field's address to C for the length of one call (§4.19), and a lend may stand only as an argument of a call — here it stands somewhere the pointer outlives the record it points into",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "pass it directly as an argument of the call that reads or fills it. The bytes belong to the record, which is a value: the slot holding it is released when its function returns, so an address kept past its call points into memory the next statement may reuse. A C function that keeps the pointer needs the bytes copied, which is what `validated_bytes` does for text"
    )
    return d

# A Heroes function whose result is `cstr` (panel 122 R3, defect 022).
#
# WHY A SIGNATURE AND NOT A FLOW ANALYSIS. `return pass(c: s.cstr())` puts the
# lend in an argument position, which the position rule blesses, and returns a
# call RESULT, which no rule about lends can see; both compiling seats built it
# and it is heap-use-after-free under the sanitizer. Refusing the SIGNATURE
# closes the whole route, and it was measured free: every `-> cstr` in this
# tree is already inside an `extern` group.
#
# The repair is the one the corpus already ships, so the note names it rather
# than guessing: answer the `str` and let the caller lend it. That is what
# `tests/golden/surface-fixtures/externroute/bind.hero` does.
function cstr_out_of_heroes(name: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "cstr_out_of_heroes",
        message: "`" + name + "` answers `cstr`, and a `cstr` is C's pointer rather than this language's string (§4.19) — only a function inside an `extern` group may answer one",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "answer `str` and let the caller lend it with `.cstr()` at the call that reads it. A `cstr` handed out of a Heroes function points into bytes this function stops owning when it returns"
    )
    return d

# A `cstr` field on a record that is not the header's (panel 122 R4).
#
# The reader's mistake differs from `cstr_out_of_heroes`, which is why this is
# its own code: there the pointer leaves through a result, here it is PARKED,
# and the repair is a different field type rather than a different signature.
# Measured at the sitting: no such field exists in this tree, and the emitter's
# `pointer_element` note used to send people here.
function cstr_in_a_record(holder: str, field: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "cstr_in_a_record",
        message: "`" + holder + "." + field + "` holds a `cstr`, and `" + holder + "` is not a record of an `extern` group — a C pointer parked in a Heroes record outlives the bytes it points into (§4.19)",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "hold a `str` in the field and lend it with `.cstr()` at the call that reads it. A group's `record` may hold a `cstr`, because there the fields are the header's and C owns the bytes"
    )
    return d

# A lease cell's name where it may not stand (M-held-bytes, panel 124 R2 to R4
# and panel 125's soundness lane).
#
# A lease is `x: cstr @ s.lease()`: a COPY of the bytes that the program owns
# and C may read until `end_lease(@x)` frees it. What makes a second release
# impossible is that the release nulls the CELL, so the pointer may live only
# in that one cell: a copy of it — `g = x`, `g: cstr @ x`, a field, a return —
# would go on pointing at the bytes after the cell was nulled, and reading it
# through C is a use-after-free `check` could not see. So the name of a lease
# cell may stand only as an argument of a call, exactly where a lend may.
function lease_escapes(name: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "lease_escapes",
        message: "`" + name + "` is a lease — a copy of the bytes that C may read until `end_lease(@" + name + ")` — and its name may stand only as an argument of a call; here it is copied, and the copy would outlive the release",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "pass `" + name + "` directly to the call that reads it. The release nulls this one cell, so a pointer that lives anywhere else keeps pointing at freed bytes"
    )
    return d

# A write to a lease cell that is not its own `end_lease`.
function lease_written(name: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "lease_written",
        message: "`" + name + "` is a lease, and the only write a lease cell takes after its declaration is `end_lease(@" + name + ")` — a value stored over it would leak the bytes it held, or free bytes nobody leased",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "declare a second cell for the second lease. One cell, one `.lease()`, one `end_lease`"
    )
    return d

# `end_lease` on something that is not a lease cell.
function end_lease_of_no_lease(span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "end_lease_of_no_lease",
        message: "`end_lease` frees what `.lease()` made, and it takes the cell that holds it: `end_lease(@x)` where `x: cstr @ s.lease()` — this argument is not such a cell",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "a lend (`.cstr()`), a pointer from C and a literal are freed by whoever owns them, and this program does not; a lease copied into another cell cannot be released from there, because the release must null the one cell the pointer lives in"
    )
    return d

# A `ptr` lend whose receiver is not a place (panel 164).
#
# THE LEND EXISTS SO C MAY WRITE BACK, which is what separates it from
# `validated_bytes` beside it: that one copies the bytes out and the copy is the
# answer, this one hands over the field itself. So the field has to still be
# somewhere the program can see when C returns, and a returned temporary is not.
function field_lend_needs_a_place(span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "field_lend_needs_a_place",
        message: "`.ptr()` lends a field's address to C, so the field must be one this program can still name — here the bytes belong to a value nothing holds",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "bind the record first and lend the field of the binding: `s = make()` and then `s.name.ptr()`. C may write through this pointer, and a write into a value nothing holds lands in storage the next statement reuses. To READ a field's text without holding the record, `validated_bytes` copies the bytes out and needs no place"
    )
    return d

# A `ptr` lend handed to anything but a function of an `extern` group (panel
# 166, 2026-09-19; design.md §4.19).
#
# THE HEADER IS THE ONLY PLACE THE TWO QUESTIONS ABOUT A LEND ARE ANSWERED:
# whether C writes through it — the header's `const`, which clang checks against
# the cast the root decides — and how far it reads, the extent route C declares
# on the group's parameter. A Heroes function taking `ptr` can carry neither:
# its parameter is a plain `void *`, so a lend from an immutable root failed
# inside clang at the wrapper's call at exit 2 (measured on the route's landing
# day), and a lend from a `@` root reached C with nothing checked about it.
function field_lend_needs_a_header(span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "field_lend_needs_a_header",
        message: "`.ptr()` lends a field's address to C, and it may stand only as an argument of a function declared in an `extern` group — here the callee is this program's own, and no header says whether it writes through the pointer or how far it reads",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "lend at the call that reaches C. A wrapper takes the record by value, or as `@`, and lends the field inside itself — `function total(s: Slot) -> i64` returning `slot_sum(p: s.nsap.ptr(), n: 8)` — which is the shape §4.19 prescribes as the route out of a module, and the header is asked where the lend is made"
    )
    return d

# A `ptr` lend C would write through, rooted at a name the program may not
# write (panel 166, 2026-09-19; defect 065; design.md §4.4, §4.19).
#
# THE HEADER DECIDES, PER CALL, and this compiler only carries the answer. A
# lend rooted at a `=` binding or a by-value parameter crosses as
# `const void *`; a parameter the header spells without `const` is one C may
# write through; and clang refuses the two together, against the real header,
# with a flag this compiler has shipped since panel 103. `emit/ffi_lend.hero`
# reads that refusal into this sentence. Before that sitting the lend crossed as
# `void *` from every root, and `t = make()` followed by a C fill read back
# changed at exit 0 — `spec § 5`'s *only a declared `@` name can be mutated*,
# false for that program.
#
# THE REPAIR IS A `@`, AND STILL NO `Fix`: declaring the name mutable changes
# what every other line may do to it, and whether the author meant to WRITE or
# only meant to READ through a parameter the header should have marked `const`
# is theirs to say. The note names both roads.
function field_lend_written(lend: str, root: str, callee: str, c_type: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "field_lend_written",
        message: "`" + lend + "` lends a field of `" + root + "` to `" + callee + "`, and the header declares that parameter `" + c_type + "` — C may write through it, and `" + root + "` is not a `@` name",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "§4.4: only a declared `@` name can be mutated, and a C write is a mutation. Declare `" + root + "` with `@` — a `@` cell, or a `@` parameter — and the bytes C writes land where this program reads them back. To only READ the field, lend it to a parameter the header declares `const`"
    )
    return d
